from pathlib import Path
import sys
sys.path.insert(0,str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]/'authoring'))
from engine import Author
A=Author('11');add=A.add
add('2.11-01','Pressure is perpendicular force per unit area.','Its SI unit is the pascal, equal to N/m².','Force and pressure are different quantities. The same weight spread over a larger contact area gives a smaller average pressure. A sharp edge concentrates a force onto a small area and can produce a large pressure. The force component normal to the surface is relevant; a force parallel to a surface is not substituted directly into the normal-pressure relationship.','Define pressure.','Pressure is perpendicular force per unit area.',heading='Pressure definition')
add('2.11-02','Average pressure is P = F/A.','Force must be in N and area in m² for pressure in Pa; 1 kPa = 1000 Pa.','A 60 N force over 0.020 m² gives 3000 Pa or 3 kPa. Area conversions are squared: 1 cm² = 0.0001 m². Convert a measured rectangular area either by converting both lengths first or by correctly converting the final area. Dividing by an area in cm² gives N/cm², not Pa. Rearranging gives force = pressure × area.','Calculate the pressure of 40 N acting perpendicularly over 0.020 m².','P = F/A = 40/0.020.~The pressure is 2000 Pa or 2 kPa.')
add('2.11-03','For a fixed area, pressure increases in proportion to perpendicular force.','For a fixed force, increasing the contact area decreases average pressure.','A bag’s broad strap spreads its support force and reduces pressure on a shoulder. A nail tip does the reverse by applying the driving force to a small area. State which quantity is held fixed: doubling force and area together leaves their ratio unchanged. These examples concern average contact pressure; real local distributions need not be perfectly uniform.','Explain why broad snowshoes reduce pressure on snow for the same wearer.','The wearer’s supported weight is spread over a greater area.~Since P = F/A, the same force produces lower average pressure.')
add('2.11-04','An applied pressure change is transmitted through an enclosed nearly incompressible fluid.','At the same level in an ideal hydraulic system, a larger piston area gives a larger force for the same pressure.','With P = F/A, input force F1 over area A1 gives an additional pressure transmitted to output area A2, producing F2 = F1 A2/A1 in the ideal model. A jack multiplies force but the smaller piston moves farther, so useful work is not created. Brake fluid transmits a pedal-induced pressure to wheel mechanisms. Trapped air compresses and can make the response less effective; this is an explanatory model, not a repair procedure.','Explain how a hydraulic jack can produce a greater output force.','The input pressure change is transmitted through the enclosed liquid.~It acts on a larger output area, so F = PA gives a larger force.')
add('2.11-05','Pressure increases with depth in a stationary liquid.','At the same depth increase and field strength, a denser liquid gives a larger pressure increase.','A lower hole in a water container can eject a jet more strongly because its pressure above the outside atmosphere is greater. Compare points at equal vertical depths rather than equal distances along a sloping wall. Container shape does not enter the simple hydrostatic pressure difference. Surface pressure must be added when finding an absolute pressure rather than a difference.','State how pressure changes as depth increases in a stationary liquid.','It increases with depth.')
add('2.11-06','For a stationary uniform-density liquid, Δp = ρgΔh follows from the weight of a vertical liquid column.','A column of cross-sectional area A and height Δh has volume AΔh and mass ρAΔh.','The extra force supporting this column is its weight, ρAΔhg. Divide by its area using pressure = force/area: Δp = ρAΔhg/A = ρgΔh. The cancellation explains why the pressure difference does not depend on the chosen column area. The derivation assumes static liquid, approximately constant density and gravitational field. For water of density 1000 kg/m³ over 0.40 m with g = 10 N/kg, the difference is 4000 Pa.','Derive Δp = ρgΔh using the weight of a static liquid column.','Take a column of area A and height Δh, so volume = AΔh.~Use density to obtain mass = ρAΔh.~Its weight is ρAΔhg.~Divide that force by A to obtain Δp = ρgΔh.',guidance=['Method step: volume = AΔh (1 mark).','Method step: mass = density × volume = ρAΔh (1 mark).','Method step: weight = ρAΔhg (1 mark).','Method step: divide force by A and cancel A, giving Δp = ρgΔh (1 mark).'])
A.slot_overrides={'ITEM-6118-11-007-CALC':dict(prompt='Calculate the pressure difference for a 0.40 m height of water of density 1000 kg/m³ where g = 10 N/kg.',answer='Δp = ρgΔh = 1000 × 10 × 0.40.~The difference is 4000 Pa.',guidance=None)}
add('2.11-07','Hydrostatic pressure difference is Δp = ρgΔh.','Use vertical height difference in metres and density in kg/m³ for Pa.','A difference between two submerged points is not automatically the total pressure at the lower point. If the upper point is an open surface, the expression gives pressure above atmospheric pressure. A negative upward depth change gives a decrease in pressure; reporting its magnitude without direction can hide this meaning. Use one consistent height convention.','Calculate the pressure increase 0.60 m below a water surface for ρ = 1000 kg/m³ and g = 10 N/kg.','Δp = ρgΔh = 1000 × 10 × 0.60.~The increase is 6000 Pa.')
add('2.11-08','Atmospheric pressure generally decreases as altitude increases.','A higher location has less overlying air weight per area.','Air is compressible, so the change over a large height is not represented accurately by treating the whole atmosphere as a constant-density liquid. The qualitative explanation is that fewer overlying air molecules contribute weight above a higher level. A local pressure reading also varies with weather, so altitude is not its sole influence.','State the general change in atmospheric pressure with increasing altitude.','It decreases.')
add('2.11-09','A manometer uses a liquid-level difference to measure a pressure difference.','The larger pressure pushes its connected liquid surface lower, and Δp = ρgΔh uses the vertical separation of levels.','For a U-tube with one arm open, compare the gas side with atmospheric pressure. If the gas pushes its side down, its pressure exceeds atmosphere by the calculated liquid-column difference. If its side is higher, the gas pressure is below atmosphere. Read both levels and subtract their heights; the length along the curved tube is irrelevant. A school demonstration should use a suitable safe liquid and low pressures, rather than mercury.','Describe how to find gas pressure relative to atmosphere with an open U-tube manometer.','Measure the vertical liquid-level difference and calculate ρgΔh; the gas pressure is higher when its connected surface is lower.')
add('2.11-10','A pressure in mm Hg describes the pressure difference supported by that height of mercury under specified conditions.','For these conversions, a supplied school approximation is 1 mm Hg ≈ 133 Pa.','Multiply a mm Hg value by the stated Pa-per-mm-Hg factor to obtain Pa. For the reverse direction, divide by that factor. For example, 50 mm Hg corresponds to approximately 6650 Pa using 133 Pa per mm Hg. The conversion is a numerical exercise, not a recommendation to use mercury in a student apparatus. State rounding consistently with the supplied factor.','Calculate the pressure in Pa corresponding to 30 mm Hg using 1 mm Hg = 133 Pa.','Pressure = 30 × 133.~The pressure is 3990 Pa.')
add('2.11-11','A pressure difference can drive air from higher toward lower pressure.','This pressure-gradient effect contributes to wind.','Real wind direction also depends on Earth’s rotation, friction and local terrain, so it need not travel in a straight line from a high-pressure centre to a low-pressure centre. For the basic model, a pressure difference across a region produces a resultant force on the air. Do not describe wind as a flow from low to high pressure caused by the pressure difference alone.','State the direction in which a pressure difference tends to drive air.','From higher toward lower pressure.')
C=A.choice
C('2.11-01','Which quantity is force per area?',('Pressure','That is its definition.'),('Density','This is mass per volume.'),('Work','This uses force times displacement.'),('Power','This is energy per time.'))
C('2.11-02','Which unit equals a pascal?',('N/m²','This is force per area.'),('N m','This is not force per area.'),('kg/m³','This measures density.'),('J/s','This measures power.'))
C('2.11-03','A fixed force acts over twice the area. What happens to pressure?',('It halves','The denominator doubles.'),('It doubles','This reverses the area dependence.'),('It quadruples','There is no area-squared relationship here.'),('It stays the same','The force-to-area ratio changes.'))
C('2.11-04','What is transmitted through an ideal hydraulic liquid?',('Applied pressure change','The same pressure change acts over different areas.'),('Equal piston force regardless of area','Force depends on area.'),('Newly created work','Input energy is required.'),('An increase in mass','No material is created.'))
C('2.11-08','At greater altitude, what generally happens to atmospheric pressure?',('It falls','There is less overlying air weight.'),('It rises due to more overlying air','There is less, not more.'),('It becomes density','These are different quantities.'),('It is fixed by container shape','The atmosphere is not controlled by a local vessel.'))
C('2.11-09','In an open U-tube, gas pushes its side lower. How does gas pressure compare with atmosphere?',('Higher','The additional pressure supports the level difference.'),('Lower','That would raise the gas-side surface.'),('Equal with a nonzero level difference','That contradicts hydrostatic balance for the same liquid.'),('Impossible to compare directions','The displaced direction provides the sign.'))
C('2.11-11','What does a pressure difference tend to do to air?',('Drive it toward lower pressure','The gradient supplies a resultant force.'),('Drive it toward higher pressure by itself','That reverses the basic direction.'),('Remove its mass','Flow does not destroy mass.'),('Stop molecular motion','Air molecules remain in motion.'))
C('2.11-02','What pressure is 30 N over 0.010 m²?',('3000 Pa','Divide force by area.'),('0.30 Pa','This multiplies force and area.'),('3 Pa','This misplaces three powers of ten.'),('300 Pa','This is ten times too small.'))
C('2.11-07','For ρ = 1000 kg/m³ and g = 10 N/kg, what is Δp over 0.20 m?',('2000 Pa','Use ρgΔh.'),('200 Pa','This omits g.'),('50 000 Pa','This divides by depth.'),('20 Pa','This omits the density scale.'))
C('2.11-10','Using 133 Pa per mm Hg, what is 20 mm Hg?',('2660 Pa','Multiply by 133.'),('6.65 Pa','This divides the conversion factor by the reading.'),('153 Pa','This adds unlike values.'),('0.150 Pa','This reverses the conversion.'))
C('2.11-04','At the same pressure, output piston area is three times input area. What is ideal output force?',('Three times input force','F = PA with common pressure.'),('One third input force','This reverses the area effect.'),('Equal input force','The areas differ.'),('Nine times input force','Force is proportional to area, not its square.'))
C('2.11-02','What is 4 kPa in Pa?',('4000 Pa','The prefix kilo means one thousand.'),('0.004 Pa','This converts in the wrong direction.'),('400 Pa','This uses a factor of one hundred.'),('40 Pa','This uses a factor of ten.'))
