P('2.11-01','State the SI unit of pressure.','Pascal, Pa.',1,'One mark for Pa.')
P('2.11-05','State how increasing liquid density changes pressure at a fixed depth and g.','It increases the pressure above the surface value.',1,'One mark for increased pressure difference.')
P('2.11-04','Explain the force multiplication in an ideal hydraulic jack.','The same pressure change acts on the output piston. Its larger area gives a larger force through F = PA.',2,'One mark for pressure transmission; one for larger area producing larger force.')
P('2.11-07','Calculate water pressure above atmosphere at 0.35 m depth using ρ = 1000 kg/m³ and g = 10 N/kg.','Δp = ρgh = 3500 Pa.',2,'One mark for ρgh; one for 3500 Pa.')
P('2.11-02','Calculate the force on 0.020 m² subject to 3500 Pa additional pressure.','F = PA = 70 N.',2,'One mark for pressure times area; one for 70 N.')
A.practical=dict(graph='Plot pressure difference vertically against depth horizontally; subtract surface pressure if the sensor reads absolute pressure.',unit='Pa/m',meaning='The pressure-depth gradient is ρg for a static liquid of uniform density, not density alone. Divide it by g to infer density.',method=['Zero a suitable low-range pressure sensor at the water surface.','Measure depth vertically from the free surface to the sensing point.','Lower the sensing point to a chosen depth and wait for the reading to settle.','Record pressure difference and repeat for several depths without moving the surface reference.','Repeat readings while raising the sensor and check for a zero shift.'],control='Keep the liquid and its temperature similar, keep the sensor orientation fixed, remove bubbles and prevent electrical equipment contacting spilled water.',table='Record depth / m, pressure reading / Pa and pressure above the surface reading / Pa.',conclusion='A linear pressure-depth graph supports the static-liquid relationship; its slope gives ρg and can be compared with the chosen liquid and g.')
